<Page title="Start here" eyebrow="Orientation" description="What FBA Tactics covers, how to read a page, and a suggested reading order for sellers who import products and sell them on US marketplaces." lede="This site answers two questions for sellers who import: what will the marketplace take from each sale, and what does US Customs require before the goods can be sold. Here is the order we suggest reading it in.">
  <Flow title="Suggested reading order (site diagram)" steps={[{ t: 'Know your fees', d: 'Platform fee pages' }, { t: 'Know your importer role', d: 'Who files, who pays' }, { t: 'Classify and value', d: 'The duty base and rate' }, { t: 'Stack the costs', d: 'Duty plus entry fees' }, { t: 'Stay compliant', d: 'Marking, certificates, hazmat' }]} />
  <h2>How to read a page here</h2>
  <p>Each guide opens with a short summary, then explains the rule, works through the arithmetic where there is any, and ends with "What to check yourself" — the steps to confirm the point on the official source for your own goods. Numbers in square brackets link to the source list at the bottom of the page. Every source is the agency or platform that sets the rule, and shows the date it was checked. If you see "Last verified" with an old date, treat the figures as a starting point and check the source.</p>
  <h2>1. Start with the fees you will pay on every sale</h2>
  <p>Read the fee reference for the platform you sell on — <a href="/marketplaces/amazon/">Amazon</a>, <a href="/marketplaces/walmart/">Walmart</a>, <a href="/marketplaces/ebay/">eBay</a>, <a href="/marketplaces/etsy/">Etsy</a> or <a href="/marketplaces/shopify/">Shopify</a> — then put your own price and cost into the <a href="/tools/margin-calculator/">margin calculator</a>. If you are weighing a second channel, <a href="/guides/ebay-etsy-for-amazon-sellers/">the same $25 item on five platforms</a> shows the arithmetic side by side.</p>
  <h2>2. Work out who the importer is</h2>
  <p>Whoever is the importer of record owns the duty bill and the compliance obligations. <a href="/guides/incoterms-who-is-importer/">Who can be the importer of record</a> explains the rule; <a href="/guides/entry-type-11-vs-01/">informal versus formal entry</a> and <a href="/guides/customs-bonds/">customs bonds</a> explain the paperwork that follows.</p>
  <h2>3. Get the duty base right</h2>
  <p>Duty is the value multiplied by a rate. The rate comes from <a href="/guides/hts-classification-basics/">classification</a> and <a href="/guides/country-of-origin-sourcing/">country of origin</a>; the value from <a href="/guides/first-sale-valuation/">transaction value rules</a>. Then see <a href="/guides/tariff-stack-2026/">how additional duties and entry fees stack</a> and estimate yours with the <a href="/tools/tariff-stack-estimator/">duty and fee estimator</a>.</p>
  <h2>4. Avoid the expensive surprises</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/origin-marking-requirements/">Country-of-origin marking</a>, <a href="/guides/customs-holds-and-exams/">exams and detentions</a>, <a href="/guides/antidumping-duties/">antidumping duties</a>, <a href="/guides/us-product-compliance/">product certificates</a> and <a href="/guides/hazmat-dangerous-goods/">hazardous materials rules</a> are where shipments get held or fined.</p>
  <h2>5. Recover what you can and keep clean books</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/duty-drawback/">Drawback</a>, <a href="/guides/foreign-trade-zones/">foreign-trade zones</a> and <a href="/guides/prior-disclosure-protests/">protests</a> are the routes to getting duty back or correcting an entry. <a href="/guides/accrual-accounting-cogs/">Inventory and cost of goods sold</a> covers how those costs reach your tax return.</p>
  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>When you have read a few guides, the <a href="/quiz/">12-question knowledge check</a> tests rules covered in these guides. Each answer cites its source.</p>
</Page>
